7. WHY YESTERDAY WORKED AND TODAY FAILED
============================================================
Yesterday table had 5 columns:
  CREATE TABLE loan_table (id INTEGER, name TEXT, cibil INT, salary INT, city TEXT);
  -> INSERT with 5 values worked: VALUES (1, 'Hyarel', 750, 80000, 'Mumbai');

Today table has 2 columns:
  CREATE TABLE loan_table (name TEXT, cibil INT);
  -> Same 5-values INSERT fails today.

Same command works in one table, fails in another table, because table definition changed.
Also different .db file = different notebook. Yesterday records are in yesterday file, not in today file my_loan.db.

Check files:
  ls -l *.db
  sqlite3 loan.db   -> Yesterday file
  sqlite3 my_loan.db -> Today file

============================================================
8. CRUD - CREATE, READ, UPDATE, DELETE
============================================================
CREATE - Make table:
  CREATE TABLE loan_table (name TEXT, cibil INT);

READ - See data:
  SELECT * FROM loan_table;
  SELECT * FROM loan_table WHERE name = 'Hyarel';

INSERT - Add record:
  INSERT INTO loan_table VALUES ('Hyarel', 750);
  INSERT INTO loan_table (name, cibil) VALUES ('Hyarel', 750);

UPDATE - Modify CIBIL number (CIBIL, not civil):
  UPDATE loan_table SET cibil = 780 WHERE name = 'Hyarel';
  Never UPDATE without WHERE, otherwise all records become 780.
  Before UPDATE, always do SELECT with same WHERE to confirm person.

DELETE - Remove one record:
  DELETE FROM loan_table WHERE name = 'Rahul';
  Never DELETE without WHERE, otherwise all records deleted.
  Before DELETE, always do SELECT with same WHERE to confirm person.

DROP - Remove full table:
  DROP TABLE loan_table;
  Table + data both gone.

============================================================
9. GOLDEN RULES
============================================================
1. Every command ends with ; before you press Enter.
2. If you see ...>, type ; and press Enter to clear, or .exit and login again.
3. Do NOT type new command while still in ...>, old + new will join and give wrong error.
4. No output after SELECT = Table is empty, not error. Check with SELECT COUNT(*).
5. No output after INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE = Success, command worked.
6. Before UPDATE / DELETE, always do SELECT with same WHERE.
7. 1 INSERT = 1 Record = 2 Values for this table.
8. 5 Records = 5 INSERTs.
9. Count commas inside ( ): 1 comma = 2 values = Correct.
10. Single quotes ' ' for name, no quotes for number. Example: ('Hyarel', 750)

